= Tool use (artificial intelligence) =

== Overview ==
In artificial intelligence, '''tool use''' is the ability of a model or agent system to select, invoke, and incorporate external functions or resources while solving a task.

Tools can extend a system beyond information or computation available directly inside the model.

== Examples of tools ==
AI tools can include:

* search engines;
* databases;
* calculators;
* code interpreters;
* application programming interfaces;
* software commands;
* external models;
* browsers;
* files;
* robotic actuators or devices.

The exact meaning of “tool” depends on the system boundary.

== Tool-use pipeline ==
A tool-using system may need to:

<pre>
identify need for a tool
→ discover or select tool
→ construct arguments
→ invoke tool
→ receive execution result
→ interpret result
→ continue task
</pre>

Longer tasks can require multiple tools and repeated execution feedback.

== Function calling ==
Structured function calling is one common implementation mechanism.

However:

<pre>
tool use ≠ function calling only
</pre>

Tools can be invoked through code, commands, language interfaces, software automation, robotics interfaces, or other action protocols.

== Tool use and agents ==
A model can make one tool call without functioning as a full agent.

Agent systems often combine tool use with planning, memory/state, iterative observation, and action selection.

== Reliability ==
Tool use introduces additional failure modes:

* choosing the wrong tool;
* incorrect arguments;
* stale or untrusted outputs;
* permission errors;
* cascading multi-step failures;
* tool-side side effects;
* unsafe actions.

Correct tool invocation does not guarantee correct overall behavior.

